# 🛡️ {{ project_name }} - Guardian Security Service

Policy enforcement for {{ project_name }}: the Guardian checks requests against Rego policies evaluated by Open Policy Agent (OPA).

## 🔧 Configuration

### Environment Variables

```bash
AGENT_HOST={{ guardian_host | default('0.0.0.0') }}
AGENT_PORT={{ guardian_port | default('11438') }}
OPA_URL=http://{{ project_dir }}-opa:8181
MAX_RISK_THRESHOLD={{ max_risk_threshold | default('0.7') }}
```

### Ports

- **{{ guardian_port | default('11438') }}**: Guardian (A2A agent)
- **8181**: OPA policy server

## 📡 Endpoints

- `GET /health` - Health check
- `GET /.well-known/agent-card.json` - Agent card
- `POST /` - A2A endpoint: other agents send validation requests here

```bash
curl http://localhost:{{ guardian_port | default('11438') }}/health
```

## 🔒 Policies

Policies live in `./services/guardian/opa/policies/` and OPA loads that folder at start-up:

- `semantic_access.rego` - who may call which Semantic Layer tool
- `a2a_access.rego` - which agents may talk to which
- `basic.rego` - base rules

Add a policy of your own with:

```bash
abi-core add policies --name <name> --domain <domain>
```

It is written to the same folder in the `abi.custom` package, which the Guardian evaluates. Restart OPA to load it:

```bash
docker compose restart {{ project_dir }}-opa
```

## 🛠️ Development

```bash
docker compose up {{ project_dir }}-opa {{ project_dir }}-guardian
docker compose logs -f {{ project_dir }}-guardian
docker compose logs -f {{ project_dir }}-opa   # OPA decision logs
```

